= 24 July 1974 — The airport confrontation

[? if cyprus_campaign_version == 1 : [+ cyprus_campaign_report +] ?]

Historical source reference:

Callaghan had made the incident a matter of prestige. In this way, he could both give the passive British a role to play and “save” the airport, which had great symbolic value. As Turkish commanders pressed the peacekeeping forces, made up of British troops, to “leave the airport,” Secretary-General Waldheim not only telephoned Ecevit but then went to Washington and asked Kissinger to help on the matter...

NATO Secretary-General Luns telephoned Deputy Permanent Representative Turgut Tülümen in Brussels and said, “Please do not take the airport. It turns out it is not in your hands, as you have claimed. If you advance, there may be a clash with the British in the peacekeeping force.”

The airport really was not in our hands. When it had been surrounded, a message saying “it is ours” had been sent... The situation could not be understood with certainty.

Mehmet Ali Birand, 30 Sıcak Gün, PDF page 261 — English translation of the source excerpt.


- @historical: Continue limited expansion, promising not to seize the airport by force.
- @alternative: Accept UN control, negotiate monitored access and freeze further advances.
- @pressure: Continue expansion and maintain the demand for airport control.

